○ ob-8qt.4 · bf16/fp16 recurrent-state variant of the GDN kernels   [● P2 · OPEN]
Owner: Claude · Type: task
Created: 2026-08-02 · Updated: 2026-08-02

DESCRIPTION

  Attacks the actual GDN decode bottleneck rather than a proxy for it.        
                                                                              
  CORRECTED FIGURES 2026-08-02 (read from Qwen3.5-4B config.json, see         
  docs/adr/0003): per-layer recurrent state is n_value_heads x d_k x d_v = 32 
  x 128 x 128 = 524,288 floats = 2.0MB fp32, which is 48MB across the 24 GDN  
  layers - DOUBLE the 262,144 figure previously quoted here. That earlier     
  number came from the GDN-2 paper's 1.3B config and happens to match Qwen3.5-
  0.8B (16 value heads), not the 4B. State is read AND written every token, so
  bf16 halves ~96MB/token of traffic to ~48MB.                                
                                                                              
  Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, so kernel cleverness cannot help it;      
  narrowing the state can. This is the one lever that moves decode.           
                                                                              
  Add bf16/fp16 state variants of gdn_gated_scan and gdn_cumdecay. CRITICAL   
  PRECISION CONSTRAINT already established: the DECAY ACCUMULATOR MUST STAY   
  FP32 regardless - a decay of 0.5 compounded over 64 steps is ~5e-20 and     
  underflows fp16 outright. Mixed precision (narrow state, wide accumulate),  
  not uniform narrowing.                                                      
                                                                              
  This is also where SVE2 finally earns its keep over SVE1: widening multiply-
  accumulates (svmlalb/svmlalt) and bf16 dot. The fp32 kernels needed only    
  base SVE.                                                                   
                                                                              
  Gate on the correctness oracle with long-context runs, since recurrent-state
  error accumulates over the sequence rather than staying local.              



NOTES

  RE-SCOPED 2026-08-02 after computing the decode traffic breakdown           
  (docs/METRICS.md appendix). Earlier framing of this bead as 'the one lever  
  that moves decode' was WRONG.                                               
                                                                              
  At 100GB/s the traffic split per decode token for Qwen3.5-4B is: weights    
  7.5GiB at fp16 or 1.9GiB at INT4, versus GDN state 96MiB fp32 across 24     
  layers. So state is ~5% of decode traffic at INT4 weights and ~1% at fp16.  
  Halving it to bf16 buys ~2-3%, not a step change. The dominant decode lever 
  is WEIGHT QUANTIZATION (INT4 takes decode from ~12.5 to ~50 tok/s by this   
  model), which is ob-qpa/ob-onz territory and where KleidiAI's INT4/i8mm GEMV
  kernels matter.                                                             
                                                                              
  Still worth doing, but as a MEMORY optimization: halving resident state     
  (48MB -> 24MB across 24 layers) helps fit long context alongside weights.   
  Dropped from P1 to P2 accordingly, and the precision constraint stands - the
  decay accumulator must remain fp32.                                         



