○ ob-t3b.5 · Cheap check: does the RK3588 NPU toolchain also reject a runtime-length recurrence?   [● P2 · OPEN]
Owner: Claude · Type: task
Created: 2026-08-02 · Updated: 2026-08-02

DESCRIPTION

  A one-day generalisation check on the project's headline finding, made      
  possible by the RK3588 having its own ~6 TOPS NPU (Rockchip RKNN).          
                                                                              
  We established that the CIX NOE Compiler cannot express a runtime-length    
  sequential recurrence: Scan rejected, Loop only via static unrolling, Loop  
  with runtime trip count rejected (docs/FINDINGS.md section 1). If RKNN - an 
  entirely independent vendor toolchain - fails the same way, the finding     
  stops being 'a CIX limitation' and becomes 'edge NPU toolchains generally   
  cannot host a linear-attention recurrence'. That is a much stronger and more
  reusable claim, and it directly strengthens the Potential Impact argument.  
                                                                              
  Method is already built: feed the SAME seven ONNX probe graphs in           
  artifacts/npu_op_probe/ to the RKNN toolkit and record what it accepts,     
  silently falls back on, or rejects. The probes are hand-authored and pre-   
  verified under onnxruntime, so a rejection is attributable to the toolchain 
  rather than a malformed graph.                                              
                                                                              
  Strictly time-boxed: if RKNN setup exceeds a day, abandon and record that   
  instead. The submission is about Arm, not Rockchip, and a negative on effort
  spent is not worth losing core work over.                                   



