### Device
- NVIDIA Tegra X1, Cortex-A57 (Armv8.0-A), 4 cores @ 1479 MHz
- NEON only — no SVE, no dotprod, no i8mm, no bf16
- LPDDR4 64-bit @ 1600 MHz → 12.8 GiB/s peak
- Most constrained device in the fleet — proves kernels are genuinely portable

### Optimization stack (cumulative, Qwen3.5-4B seq=64)

| Optimization | Bead | cumdecay | gated_scan | conv |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Baseline (1 thread, NEON 4-wide) | — | 2108 μs | 3893 μs | 1961 μs |
| + Mixed-precision (fp16/bf16 state) | ob-8qt.4 | — | — | — |
| + OpenMP (4 cores) | ob-8qt.5 | 829 μs (2.5×) | 1501 μs (2.6×) | 912 μs (2.1×) |
| + NEON 8-wide unroll (scan/decay) | ob-8qt.6 | 526 μs (4.0×) | 1003 μs (3.9×) | 901 μs (2.2×) |
| + NEON 8-wide unroll (conv) | ob-8qt.7 | 510 μs (**4.1×**) | 1010 μs (**3.9×**) | 590 μs (**3.3×**) |

### Decode-phase results (ob-8qt.9)
Optimized kernels also accelerate decode (seq=1): 1.7–3× over baseline.
Narrow-format penalty halved by OpenMP. OpenMP fork/join overhead (~2 μs)
dominates at decode — serial decode path recommended for future work.

### Sustained-load (120s)
Optimized kernels achieve 2.8 GiB/s sustained (3.6× baseline). No hard throttling;
gentle ~5% throughput decline after 80s as temperature reaches 58°C — invisible
with baseline kernels that didn't push the device hard enough.

